this is gonna be a bit of a bold  statement but I personally believe the  coronavirus has allowed people to play  the lazy card what's going on guys my  name is David Tomich and welcome back to  the channel today we're talking about  the 2019 MacBook Pro versus the 2020  MacBook Pro and why I believe Apple has  copped out on developing what could have  been such a great product 20/20 should  have been one of the biggest years for  technology it was lined up to be a  history-making a decade 2019 set the  standard in folding tech and new gadgets  that we'd never seen before  if 2020 were to play out the correct way  without the global pandemic we probably  would have seen a much bigger demand and  a much greater leap in technology this  year unfortunately it has given people  with the opportunity to sit back and go  know from a business point of view we're  not going to put the best products out  there in the market we're not going to  make sure we are the leaders in the  industry instead we're going to take a  step back and go people are gonna buy  our stuff because that's who we are this  is my belief for the 2020 MacBook Pro  anyway especially when you compare the  base model 2020 to the base model 2019  so one of the most interesting features  about a new MacBook Pro is what happens  to the pricing model how Apple decides  where it's going to place it in the rank  of all its other products if we take a  look at the last decade of macbook  pricing we're gonna see a very  interesting graph appear so in 2010 the  MacBook Pro came in at seventeen hundred  and ninety nine Australian dollars or  eleven ninety nine USD it followed that  trend in 2011 and 2012 as well but in  2012 it introduced a Retina display as  well which bumped it up to sixteen  hundred and ninety nine USD or $24.99  AUD in 2013 it had a huge price bump for  two thousand two hundred and ninety nine  Australian dollars or fourteen hundred  and  99 USD as his base model then drop back  down all of a sudden surprisingly to  nineteen hundred and ninety nine dollars  for the introductory MacBook Pro 13 inch  model in 2015 it kept this price point  until 2016 they introduced the touch bar  it left back up to two thousand two  hundred and ninety nine Australian  dollars for the model without the touch  bar or 2699 with the touch bar when 2018  came in they started phasing out the  touch bar secretly by introducing the  two model system so the model without  the touch bar dropped to one thousand  nine hundred and ninety nine dollars but  the model with the touch bar remained  the same price at two thousand six  hundred and ninety nine dollars in 2019  we saw that price nosedive a little bit  because they removed the secondary  option without the touch bar and it led  to a price point of 1999 Australian  starting which is what it is today in  2020 it's interesting to note how the  economic periods have affected this  company and how it's affected their  starting price we've started at  seventeen hundred and ninety nine  dollars for their flagship top of the  range peaked out at two thousand three  hundred for their entry up model and  then drop back down to 1999 so a laptop  now a flagship laptop which has most of  the parts from a year ago model is still  the same price as a year ago is model  that gets me into the topic and theme of  this video today the 2019 MacBook Pro  and the 2020 20 MacBook Pro are very  similar devices from a base model point  of view both have the touch bar and  touch ID as standard both have an Intel  i5 core processor  as standard both have the 13 inch screen  as standard both have the Retina display  eight gigabytes of RAM two Thunderbolt  three ports and ten hours of battery  life as standard where they start to  differentiate is the 2020 model offers  more storage as standard than the 2019  model so instead of having to purchase  the upgraded model with two hundred and  56 gigabytes versus the 128 gigabytes is  standard in the 2019 model the 2020  model now comes with 256 gigabytes of  storage as standard the 2020 model is  for some reason 0.7 millimeters thicker  than the 2019 model and 30 grams heavier  than the 2019 model this isn't a very  significant difference you probably  won't notice this in day-to-day life so  it wouldn't be something that I would go  and say I know it's a huge difference  don't buy it from this fact now one of  the biggest and best changes to this  keyboard is a simple fact they've  reintroduced the Escape key as a  physical button if you're somebody like  me who hits that button at least a  million times a day it is definitely a  nice inclusion to see in the 2020  MacBook Pro where it does start to get a  little bit different and where it does  start to justify a new model and new  introduction is the simple change in the  keyboard setup the 2020 MacBook Pro  comes with a scissor switch keyboard  setup this is the old butterfly switch  keyboard if you've used a 20-19 or  previous MacBook Pro you're going to  notice that the keyboard isn't too nice  to use and it feels like you just happen  on aluminium the whole time whereas the  new 2020 MacBook keyboard has your  traditional scissor switches which feels  like it has a little bit more travel and  is a nicer product at the end of the day  the difference between scissor switches  and butterfly switches is scissor  switches do what you think there's two  little plastic blades inside that go up  and down as the key pushes versus  butterfly switches which do the opposite  like butterfly wings do now if you were  to walk into a shop today and see the  2019 MacBook Pro and the 2020 MacBook  Pro side-by-side at the exact same price  you would definitely purchase the  brand-new 2020 model thanks to the  upgrade of the keyboard but on the other  hand if you were to walk into that store  and the 2019 model was significantly  discounted which it's more than likely  going to be and you are okay with  how that keyboard feels then personally  in my opinion I would definitely go with  the 2019 MacBook Pro because it's an  identical machine  there isn't any major changes except  that keyboard that is going to throw you  off that's all from me today guys thank  you so much for watching and more  importantly thank you so much for  subscribing yesterday we hit 2,000  subscribers on the channel it isn't a  lot but it's a lot more than we started  with so thank you so much  it really means a lot to me and as  always I'll see you next Monday

